{"type":"message_end","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_01a06ddbfc207293a0fbea6a18885c38","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[\n  {\n    \"id\": \"canopy-tester-empty-inventory-rendering\",\n    \"title\": \"Zero-instance empty inventory page and fragment rendering lack regression cover\",\n    \"spec\": \"## Problem\\n\\nCanopy supports a runtime with no configured or discovered instances, but its page and fragment routes are only tested with one configured instance. The empty operator surface can regress to a panic, 500, or broken template unnoticed.\\n\\n## Required behavior\\n\\nWith `NewApp(Inventory{Instances: []}, ...)` and real templates:\\n\\n- `GET /` returns 200 and renders the fleet and selected-instance empty states.\\n- `GET /fragments/fleet` returns 200 and renders `No instances configured.`\\n- `GET /fragments/instance` returns 200 and renders `No instance selected` plus the instruction to add an instance.\\n- No route starts discovery, dereferences a missing selection, or requires host-specific Forest state.\\n\\nThis is test-only work; do not change empty-inventory behavior unless the scenario exposes a real defect.\\n\\n## Proof\\n\\nA table-driven HTTP test exercises all three routes with the actual embedded templates and an empty in-memory inventory, asserting status 200 and route-specific empty-state text. The focused server tests pass without systemd, Forest binaries, or network fixtures.\",\n    \"repo\": \"misty-step/canopy\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"lease\": null,\n    \"ask\": null,\n    \"proof\": null,\n    \"abandoned\": false,\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"at\": \"2026-09-04T18:58:37.391Z\",\n        \"by\": \"forest-misty-step/iron-forest\",\n        \"text\": \"filed-by: forest-misty-step/iron-forest @ misty-step/canopy\\ndeployment: canopy a101e023933a247460f9828b7cb8d3fce3a681ac\\nSurface: server.go:49 (handlePage), server.go:82 (handleInstance), templates/fleet.html:36, templates/instance.html:145. Behaviors: when Canopy runs with zero configured or discovered instances, GET /, GET /fragments/fleet, and GET /fragments/instance must return HTTP 200 and gracefully render the empty-state templates (\\\"No instances configured.\\\", \\\"No instance selected\\\", \\\"Add an instance to the inventory to begin observing it.\\\") without panic, nil pointer dereference, or HTTP 500. Failing example: initialize NewApp with Inventory{Instances: []} and issue GET /, GET /fragments/fleet, and GET /fragments/instance; today all tests in server_test.go initialize testInventory() with one pre-configured instance, leaving the zero-instance empty template rendering path unexercised. Acceptance: a Builder adds regression tests in server_test.go with an empty inventory asserting that GET /, GET /fragments/fleet, and GET /fragments/instance return HTTP 200, contain the expected empty-state text (\\\"No instances configured\\\", \\\"No instance selected\\\"), and do not produce template execution errors. Observed: templates have explicit branches for empty instances (fleet.html:36, instance.html:145), but grep shows no test in server_test.go exercises an empty App inventory. Required: HTTP handler regression tests for empty-inventory page and fragment rendering. Proposed test-work: add server tests verifying that GET /, /fragments/fleet, and /fragments/instance return 200 with empty-state messages when no instances are configured.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"created_by\": \"none\",\n    \"promoted_by\": \"none\",\n    \"promoted_at\": \"2026-09-04T19:06:06.276Z\",\n    \"promotions\": [\n      {\n        \"at\": \"2026-09-04T19:06:06.276Z\",\n        \"by\": \"none\",\n        \"spec\": \"## Problem\\n\\nCanopy supports a runtime with no configured or discovered instances, but its page and fragment routes are only tested with one configured instance. The empty operator surface can regress to a panic, 500, or broken template unnoticed.\\n\\n## Required behavior\\n\\nWith `NewApp(Inventory{Instances: []}, ...)` and real templates:\\n\\n- `GET /` returns 200 and renders the fleet and selected-instance empty states.\\n- `GET /fragments/fleet` returns 200 and renders `No instances configured.`\\n- `GET /fragments/instance` returns 200 and renders `No instance selected` plus the instruction to add an instance.\\n- No route starts discovery, dereferences a missing selection, or requires host-specific Forest state.\\n\\nThis is test-only work; do not change empty-inventory behavior unless the scenario exposes a real defect.\\n\\n## Proof\\n\\nA table-driven HTTP test exercises all three routes with the actual embedded templates and an empty in-memory inventory, asserting status 200 and route-specific empty-state text. The focused server tests pass without systemd, Forest binaries, or network fixtures.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-09-04T18:58:32.84Z\",\n    \"updated_at\": \"2026-09-04T19:06:06.276Z\",\n    \"derived\": {\n      \"terminal\": false,\n      \"waiting\": false,\n      \"open\": true,\n      \"live\": false,\n      \"takeable\": true\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"canopy-tester-audit-divergence-stale\",\n    \"title\": \"Audit master commit divergence stale trust override lacks regression cover\",\n    \"spec\": \"## Problem\\n\\nCanopy intentionally overrides a passing audit result to stale when the audited primary revision differs from the current primary revision. No observable test pins that trust signal.\\n\\n## Required behavior\\n\\n- With `LastResult` pass/trusted and non-empty unequal `AuditedMaster` and `LastMaster`, the selected-instance audit badge uses the `stale` signal class.\\n- Preserve the reported pass/trusted text; stale describes revision applicability, not a rewritten audit result.\\n- Equal revisions, or a missing comparison revision, retain the result-derived class.\\n- Do not change audit classification policy in this job.\\n\\n## Proof\\n\\nExercise `GET /fragments/instance?instance=one` with real templates and a seeded successful snapshot. Table cases cover unequal, equal, and missing revision refs. Assert 200, the rendered audit badge class, and preserved result text. Avoid source-text assertions against `auditView` internals.\",\n    \"repo\": \"misty-step/canopy\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"lease\": null,\n    \"ask\": null,\n    \"proof\": null,\n    \"abandoned\": false,\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"at\": \"2026-09-04T18:58:47.383Z\",\n        \"by\": \"forest-misty-step/iron-forest\",\n        \"text\": \"filed-by: forest-misty-step/iron-forest @ misty-step/canopy\\ndeployment: canopy a101e023933a247460f9828b7cb8d3fce3a681ac\\nSurface: view.go:319 (auditView), templates/instance.html:40 (Audit trust metric card). Behaviors: when an instance's audited commit (AuditedMaster) differs from its current master commit (LastMaster) — indicating new commits have landed since the last audit — Canopy must classify the audit trust class as \\\"stale\\\" (signal class \\\"stale\\\") even if LastResult was \\\"pass\\\" or \\\"trusted\\\". Failing example: construct an AuditData with LastResult = \\\"pass\\\", LastMaster = \\\"sha-new\\\", and AuditedMaster = \\\"sha-old\\\", then invoke auditView or render instance.html; today no test in the repository exercises this divergence branch, leaving the critical \\\"is audited primary trustworthy?\\\" signal unpinned. Acceptance: a Builder adds a regression test proving that auditView produces TrustClass: \\\"stale\\\" when AuditedMaster and LastMaster are non-empty and unequal despite a passing LastResult, and that instance.html renders class=\\\"signal stale\\\" with the trust value. Observed: view.go:319 contains the explicit branch, but grep over all *_test.go files confirms AuditedMaster is never referenced in tests. Required: a regression test for the audit master divergence stale-override rule. Proposed test-work: add unit/template tests verifying that master revision divergence forces audit trust class to stale.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"created_by\": \"none\",\n    \"promoted_by\": \"none\",\n    \"promoted_at\": \"2026-09-04T19:06:06.292Z\",\n    \"promotions\": [\n      {\n        \"at\": \"2026-09-04T19:06:06.292Z\",\n        \"by\": \"none\",\n        \"spec\": \"## Problem\\n\\nCanopy intentionally overrides a passing audit result to stale when the audited primary revision differs from the current primary revision. No observable test pins that trust signal.\\n\\n## Required behavior\\n\\n- With `LastResult` pass/trusted and non-empty unequal `AuditedMaster` and `LastMaster`, the selected-instance audit badge uses the `stale` signal class.\\n- Preserve the reported pass/trusted text; stale describes revision applicability, not a rewritten audit result.\\n- Equal revisions, or a missing comparison revision, retain the result-derived class.\\n- Do not change audit classification policy in this job.\\n\\n## Proof\\n\\nExercise `GET /fragments/instance?instance=one` with real templates and a seeded successful snapshot. Table cases cover unequal, equal, and missing revision refs. Assert 200, the rendered audit badge class, and preserved result text. Avoid source-text assertions against `auditView` internals.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-09-04T18:58:42.895Z\",\n    \"updated_at\": \"2026-09-04T19:06:06.292Z\",\n    \"derived\": {\n      \"terminal\": false,\n      \"waiting\": false,\n      \"open\": true,\n      \"live\": false,\n      \"takeable\": true\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"canopy-tester-initial-collection-failure\",\n    \"title\": \"Initial instance collection failure unknown freshness and attempted state lack regression cover\",\n    \"spec\": \"## Problem\\n\\nTests cover a collection failure only after a successful snapshot. They do not defend the stronger first-observation invariant: an instance that has never succeeded is unknown, never fresh or stale, while still showing the failed attempt.\\n\\n## Required behavior\\n\\nAfter the first collection attempt fails for an instance with no prior snapshot:\\n\\n- Preserve no successful snapshot and zero `LastSuccess`.\\n- Record `LastAttempt` and the collector error.\\n- Render freshness as `unknown` and reachability as false.\\n- Render `LastObserved` as `attempted \\u003ctimestamp\\u003e`, surface the error, and keep the `Waiting for first observation` state.\\n- Do not start background discovery or depend on host state.\\n\\nThis is test-only work unless the scenario exposes a real defect.\\n\\n## Proof\\n\\nUse a deterministic failing collector for one `refreshOnce`, then exercise the real instance fragment/template. Assert HTTP 200, unknown/first-observation output, attempted timestamp, and visible failure; assert no fresh/stale classification. Existing success-then-failure retention coverage remains unchanged.\",\n    \"repo\": \"misty-step/canopy\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"lease\": null,\n    \"ask\": null,\n    \"proof\": null,\n    \"abandoned\": false,\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"at\": \"2026-09-04T18:59:00.096Z\",\n        \"by\": \"forest-misty-step/iron-forest\",\n        \"text\": \"filed-by: forest-misty-step/iron-forest @ misty-step/canopy\\ndeployment: canopy a101e023933a247460f9828b7cb8d3fce3a681ac\\nSurface: view.go:208-213 (instanceView), view.go:160 (classifyFreshness), templates/instance.html:18 (\\\"Waiting for first observation\\\"). Behaviors: when an instance has never completed a successful collection (e.g. host unreachable or binary failure on first launch), Canopy must maintain its freshness as \\\"unknown\\\" (never \\\"stale\\\" or \\\"fresh\\\"), set Reachable: false, record LastObserved with prefix \\\"attempted \\u003ctimestamp\\u003e\\\", surface the failure in Errors, and render the \\\"Waiting for first observation\\\" card. Failing example: configure an instance whose collector fails immediately on the first attempt without any prior successful snapshot; today TestRefreshRetainsLastSuccessfulSnapshotOnFailure in app_test.go only tests failure after an initial success (transitioning to \\\"stale\\\"), leaving the initial-failure path (view.go:210) untested. Acceptance: a Builder adds a regression test in app_test.go asserting that after an initial refresh failure, app.state(id) has Snapshot == nil, instanceView has Freshness: \\\"unknown\\\", Reachable: false, LastObserved prefixed with \\\"attempted \\\", Errors containing the collector error, and instance.html rendering the waiting-title card. Observed: view.go:210 sets \\\"attempted \\\" when Snapshot is nil and LastAttempt is non-zero, but grep shows \\\"attempted\\\" is never asserted in any test file. Required: a regression test in app_test.go for initial instance collection failure. Proposed test-work: add an app/view regression test proving an instance failing its initial collection reports unknown freshness, attempted timestamp, and the waiting card.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"created_by\": \"none\",\n    \"promoted_by\": \"none\",\n    \"promoted_at\": \"2026-09-04T19:06:06.282Z\",\n    \"promotions\": [\n      {\n        \"at\": \"2026-09-04T19:06:06.282Z\",\n        \"by\": \"none\",\n        \"spec\": \"## Problem\\n\\nTests cover a collection failure only after a successful snapshot. They do not defend the stronger first-observation invariant: an instance that has never succeeded is unknown, never fresh or stale, while still showing the failed attempt.\\n\\n## Required behavior\\n\\nAfter the first collection attempt fails for an instance with no prior snapshot:\\n\\n- Preserve no successful snapshot and zero `LastSuccess`.\\n- Record `LastAttempt` and the collector error.\\n- Render freshness as `unknown` and reachability as false.\\n- Render `LastObserved` as `attempted \\u003ctimestamp\\u003e`, surface the error, and keep the `Waiting for first observation` state.\\n- Do not start background discovery or depend on host state.\\n\\nThis is test-only work unless the scenario exposes a real defect.\\n\\n## Proof\\n\\nUse a deterministic failing collector for one `refreshOnce`, then exercise the real instance fragment/template. Assert HTTP 200, unknown/first-observation output, attempted timestamp, and visible failure; assert no fresh/stale classification. Existing success-then-failure retention coverage remains unchanged.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-09-04T18:58:54.638Z\",\n    \"updated_at\": \"2026-09-04T19:06:06.282Z\",\n    \"derived\": {\n      \"terminal\": false,\n      \"waiting\": false,\n      \"open\": true,\n      \"live\": false,\n      \"takeable\": true\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"canopy-tester-route-param-validation\",\n    \"title\": \"HTTP route instance parameter traversal and invalid identifier rejection lack regression cover\",\n    \"spec\": \"## Problem\\n\\nHTTP instance parameters use the shared route-identifier validator before lookup or command execution, but tests cover only an unknown instance and one malformed run ID. The route boundary is not pinned across instance-accepting handlers.\\n\\n## Required behavior\\n\\n- `/`, `/fragments/fleet`, and `/fragments/instance` allow a missing optional `instance`; `/logs` requires it.\\n- Present-empty or duplicate `instance` values return 400.\\n- Dot/traversal values, Unicode whitespace/control, `/` or `\\\\\\\\`, and the validator's unsafe punctuation classes return 400 before selection or collector execution.\\n- A syntactically valid unknown instance returns 404, not 400.\\n- Missing or malformed `run` on `/logs` returns 400 without calling `Collector.Logs`.\\n- A syntactically valid unknown run reaches the collector and retains the existing 200 unknown-log fragment behavior.\\n- Do not change accepted identifier policy.\\n\\n## Proof\\n\\nA table-driven handler test covers every instance-accepting route, parameter cardinality, and each malformed class at least once; every route gets a representative traversal/separator case. A recording collector proves invalid `/logs` requests make zero calls. Keep the existing valid-unknown run assertion. Focused server tests pass.\",\n    \"repo\": \"misty-step/canopy\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"lease\": null,\n    \"ask\": null,\n    \"proof\": null,\n    \"abandoned\": false,\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"at\": \"2026-09-04T18:59:11.267Z\",\n        \"by\": \"forest-misty-step/iron-forest\",\n        \"text\": \"filed-by: forest-misty-step/iron-forest @ misty-step/canopy\\ndeployment: canopy a101e023933a247460f9828b7cb8d3fce3a681ac\\nSurface: server.go:142 (instanceParam), server.go:171 (runParam), inventory.go:138 (validateRouteIdentifier), routes GET /, GET /fragments/fleet, GET /fragments/instance, GET /logs. Behaviors: query parameter values for instance must be validated before routing or execution: path traversal (\\\"..\\\", \\\".\\\"), route separators (\\\"/\\\", \\\"\\\\\\\"), control characters, and shell metacharacters must be rejected with HTTP 400 Bad Request across /, /fragments/fleet, /fragments/instance, and /logs. Failing example: invoke GET /?instance=.. or GET /fragments/instance?instance=bad%2Fid or GET /logs?instance=..\\u0026run=run-1; today server_test.go only tests run parameter rejection on /logs (TestHandlerRejectsUnknownAndMalformedRun), leaving instance parameter malformed/traversal validation unexercised on all routes. Acceptance: a Builder adds test cases in server_test.go asserting that requests with invalid instance parameters (e.g. instance=.., instance=bad/id, instance=bad;id) return HTTP 400 Bad Request across /, /fragments/fleet, /fragments/instance, and /logs. Observed: server.go:150 checks validateRouteIdentifier for instanceParam and returns errBadInstance (status 400), but server_test.go only asserts status 404 for unknown instance and tests no malformed instance parameter. Required: HTTP route regression tests for malformed and traversal instance parameters. Proposed test-work: add server regression tests asserting invalid and traversal instance query parameters return HTTP 400 across all routes.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"created_by\": \"none\",\n    \"promoted_by\": \"none\",\n    \"promoted_at\": \"2026-09-04T19:06:06.245Z\",\n    \"promotions\": [\n      {\n        \"at\": \"2026-09-04T19:06:06.245Z\",\n        \"by\": \"none\",\n        \"spec\": \"## Problem\\n\\nHTTP instance parameters use the shared route-identifier validator before lookup or command execution, but tests cover only an unknown instance and one malformed run ID. The route boundary is not pinned across instance-accepting handlers.\\n\\n## Required behavior\\n\\n- `/`, `/fragments/fleet`, and `/fragments/instance` allow a missing optional `instance`; `/logs` requires it.\\n- Present-empty or duplicate `instance` values return 400.\\n- Dot/traversal values, Unicode whitespace/control, `/` or `\\\\\\\\`, and the validator's unsafe punctuation classes return 400 before selection or collector execution.\\n- A syntactically valid unknown instance returns 404, not 400.\\n- Missing or malformed `run` on `/logs` returns 400 without calling `Collector.Logs`.\\n- A syntactically valid unknown run reaches the collector and retains the existing 200 unknown-log fragment behavior.\\n- Do not change accepted identifier policy.\\n\\n## Proof\\n\\nA table-driven handler test covers every instance-accepting route, parameter cardinality, and each malformed class at least once; every route gets a representative traversal/separator case. A recording collector proves invalid `/logs` requests make zero calls. Keep the existing valid-unknown run assertion. Focused server tests pass.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-09-04T18:59:05.643Z\",\n    \"updated_at\": \"2026-09-04T19:06:06.245Z\",\n    \"derived\": {\n      \"terminal\": false,\n      \"waiting\": false,\n      \"open\": true,\n      \"live\": false,\n      \"takeable\": true\n    }\n  }\n]\nEXIT:0\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788549530230}}
